    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our offensive engine is anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (75.15) and **Josh Naylor** (71.72), both of whom benefit from favorable matchups against pitchers with xERAs north of 4.40. **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier play despite the potential for defensive suppression by Michael King; his ability to exploit the base paths keeps his floor significantly higher than the rest of the cohort. Similarly, **James Wood** (71.07) is a locked-in start, utilizing an elite 0.429 xwOBA and the leadoff spot to maximize his run-scoring potential against Arrighetti. **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.26) and **Mickey Moniak** (64.13) round out the top tier, with **Mickey Moniak** specifically benefiting from a high-upside platoon advantage and a slow delivery from Roki Sasaki, which provides a boost to his stolen base equity.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was undoubtedly **Cole Young** (48.96), who hovered near the \"Marginal\" threshold but ultimately yielded to the higher ceiling of **Zach McKinstry** and **Jose Caballero**. While **Cole Young** offers stability, the optimizer favored the speed profiles of **Jose Caballero** and **Mickey Moniak** to satisfy our scarcity requirements for stolen bases. We are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Dalton Rushing**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Mickey Moniak**; should any of these bats move down in their respective orders, their efficiency scores will drift toward the sub-50 danger zone. We remain cautious with **Seiya Suzuki** (52.68) today, as he faces a sharp regression in his score due to an unfavorable matchup against Kremer\u2019s stingy pitching profile, despite the boost he receives from a hitter-friendly park. Keep a close eye on the weather and official batting orders to ensure **Dalton Rushing** and company aren't shuffled out of their projected spots, which could force us to pivot toward depth pieces like **Isaac Paredes** or **Travis Bazzana** if last-minute scratches occur.",
